---
// "Introducing Cadsense", written for the team's internal Slack. It follows the pitch deck: why design
// reviews are the bottleneck, the code review precedent, the gap between Onshape's checks and a
// mentor's, then each feature with a clip. Clips come from local copies of real reviews (an elevator,
// a telescoping arm, a disc transfer, a full robot), recorded frame by frame and composed with
// tooling outside the repo. The SolidWorks clip is the demo from PR #159.
import PostLayout from "../../layouts/PostLayout.astro";
import LoopVideo from "../../components/blog/LoopVideo.astro";
import ProviderSwitch from "../../components/blog/ProviderSwitch.astro";
import Scoreboard from "../../components/blog/Scoreboard.astro";
import Toc from "../../components/blog/Toc.astro";

const title = "Introducing Cadsense";
const description =
  "Automated CAD review for robot CAD, the way CodeRabbit reviews code. Claude or Codex reviews Onshape and SolidWorks models and pins what it finds to the parts.";
const img = (name: string) => `/blog/introducing-cadsense/${name}`;


// What existing CAD tools check, next to what a mentor checks in a design review.
const gap = {
  tools: ["Parts that overlap", "Clearances you set up yourself", "Mass and center of gravity"],
  mentors: [
    "Can we actually make this part with our tools?",
    "Is everything accessible?",
    "Is it durable?",
    "Does it follow our design standards?",
  ],
} as const;
---

<PostLayout title={title} description={description} image={img("telescope-poster.webp")} draft>
  <Toc />
  <article class="post">
    <header class="post-head">
      <p class="kicker">October 2026</p>
      <h1 id="intro">{title}</h1>
      <p class="dek">
        Automated CAD review.
      </p>
      <p class="byline">Advait Johari</p>
    </header>

    <p>
      Cadsense is almost ready for you to use on your own CAD. Here's how it works and what you'll need to try it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Point it at an Onshape document or a SolidWorks file and ask for a review. Claude or Codex works through the
      model the way a mentor would and pins comments to the parts they're about.
    </p>

    <figure class="wide">
      <LoopVideo src={img("telescope")} poster={img("telescope-poster.webp")} width={1920} height={1200} label="Clicking through four review comments on a telescoping arm. Each one moves the camera to the view the agent found it in." />
      <figcaption>A review of a telescoping arm. Each comment opens in the view the agent found it in.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2 id="why" data-toc-label="The Bottleneck">Design reviews are the bottleneck</h2>
    <p>
      Before final approval, a mechanism usually goes through five major revisions. Each one means sitting down
      with a mentor to review it by hand, and that has a few problems:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Mentors aren't always available.</li>
      <li>Each review takes hours of a mentor's time.</li>
      <li>Small mistakes, like bad clearances or overlapping parts, are easy to miss in a visual review.</li>
    </ul>
    <figure class="wide">
      <LoopVideo src={img("robot")} poster={img("robot-poster.webp")} width={1920} height={1200} label="A full competition robot with 1,362 components turning slowly in the Cadsense viewer." />
    </figure>

    <h2 id="precedent" data-toc-skip>Code already solved this</h2>
    <p>
      Software teams had the same bottleneck. Every change waited on a senior engineer to read it. Since 2023, tools
      like CodeRabbit and Greptile review every change automatically. They flag real bugs within minutes, and a human
      only steps in to make the final call.
    </p>
    <figure class="wide-sm">
      <div class="reviews">
        <img src={img("coderabbit.webp")} alt="A CodeRabbit review comment on a pull request: preserve legacy bare tokens that match the encoding prefix, rated Major" width="1548" height="549" loading="lazy" />
        <img src={img("greptile.webp")} alt="A Greptile review comment on the same pull request: old tokens skip objects, rated P1" width="1548" height="558" loading="lazy" />
      </div>
    </figure>
    <p>CAD has nothing like it yet.</p>

    <h2 id="gap" data-toc-skip>The gap</h2>
    <div class="gap">
      <div>
        <h3>What Onshape and similar tools check</h3>
        <ul>{gap.tools.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}</ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>What mentors check</h3>
        <ul>{gap.mentors.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}</ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>
      CAD tools catch what you tell them to look for. Mentors catch what you didn't think to ask about. Cadsense is
      built to do both.
    </p>

    <h2 id="solution" data-toc-label="Reviewing CAD like code">Cadsense reviews CAD like code</h2>
    <p>
      Open up a project and Cadsense flags problems before a mentor ever sees them: interference, missing fasteners, bad
      clearances. Mentors still sign off on the final design. They just stop spending meetings on mistakes a tool
      could catch, so you and they can focus on what really matters, the design.
    </p>
    <p>
      I ran it on an elevator cascade before its design review. It left 13 comments
      on the 361-part model. One was a blocker. The 40T gear sticks out about 1.5 mm past the carriage tube and grinds
      into a newly added cable clamp plate. Nobody had checked the plate against the gear, because the
      gear hadn't changed.
    </p>
    <figure class="wide">
      <LoopVideo src={img("comments")} poster={img("comments-poster.webp")} width={1920} height={1200} label="Clicking through comments in the Cadsense viewer on an elevator. Each one moves the camera to the view the agent found it in." />
      <figcaption>Comments from the cascade review, including two blockers from a second pass.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2 id="comments" data-toc-skip>Comments land on the part</h2>
    <p>
      The reviewer pins each comment to the exact part it's about. Click a comment and the view jumps there, with any
      parts in the way ghosted out. Each one is rated blocker, concern, question, or nit, so you know what to fix
      first.
    </p>
    <figure class="wide">
      <LoopVideo src={img("transfer")} poster={img("transfer-poster.webp")} width={1920} height={1200} label="Clicking through three comments on a disc transfer: belts in the disc's path, a hidden duplicate plate, and a bellypan inside the frame tubes." />
      <figcaption>A disc transfer. The first comment: the 11 in disc won't fit when between the belts (10.1 in apart).</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2 id="revisions" data-toc-skip>It checks what changed</h2>
    <p>
      Each time you sync a new revision, the reviewer compares it to the last one. It checks whether you fixed what it
      flagged, and it only reviews what changed. Comments on parts that moved, changed shape, or got deleted are marked
      outdated, and it can propose resolving the ones you fixed.
    </p>
    <p>Mentors work the same way. They remember what they asked you to fix, and they check that first.</p>

    <h2 id="learns" data-toc-skip>Push back, and it learns</h2>
    <p>
      You resolve, discuss, or dismiss each comment. Discuss starts a chat message about that comment so you can argue
      with it. A dismiss reason becomes a learning that every later review reads, so the same false alarm
      doesn't come back.
    </p>
    <figure class="wide">
      <LoopVideo src={img("triage")} poster={img("triage-poster.webp")} width={1920} height={1200} label="Dismissing a comment with a typed reason, then pressing Discuss on another, which drops a message about it into the chat." />
    </figure>

    <h2 id="providers" data-toc-skip>Claude or Codex</h2>
    <p>
      Reviews run on Claude Code or Codex, on whichever plan you already have, including ChatGPT's free tier. Pick the
      model per chat.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <ProviderSwitch />
    </figure>

    <h2 id="platforms" data-toc-skip>Onshape, SolidWorks, or a STEP file</h2>
    <p>
      Onshape documents sync through the Onshape API. SolidWorks parts and assemblies open through the SolidWorks
      installed on your computer.
    </p>
    <figure class="wide">
      <LoopVideo src={img("solidworks")} poster={img("solidworks-poster.webp")} width={1920} height={1200} label="Adding a project from a SolidWorks assembly on Windows. Cadsense converts it with SolidWorks and opens the model." />
    </figure>

    <section class="soon-section">
      <span class="soon-tag">Coming soon</span>
      <h2 id="rag" data-toc-skip>Grounded in what other teams built</h2>
      <p>
        The reviewer will be integrated with FRC RAG, so it can draw on 64 technical binders from 10+ years of FRC. Most
        mechanisms aren't new. When it flags a problem, it will show you how other teams already solved it. That way the feedback is grounded in what real teams built, not what an AI guesses should
        work.
      </p>
      <figure class="narrow">
          <img src={img("frc-rag.webp")} alt="An FRC RAG answer about how teams transfer game pieces from an intake to a shooter, citing a team's 2020 design" width="636" height="706" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>FRC RAG on its own today, answering how teams transfer game pieces from an intake to a shooter.</figcaption>
      </figure>

    </section>

    <h2 id="how">How it works</h2>
    <p>
      The agent works the viewer the way you would. It finds parts, ghosts and isolates them, cuts sections, explodes
      the assembly, and measures gaps. It checks each part against the design standards and your comments from
      earlier reviews, then reports what it found on the parts themselves.
    </p>
    <figure class="wide">
      <LoopVideo src={img("tools")} poster={img("tools-poster.webp")} width={1920} height={1200} label="In the viewer, ghosting the whole assembly, highlighting the gearbox block, then exploding the assembly and putting it back." />
    </figure>
    <p>While it works, the chat shows short notes and a thumbnail of every view it checked.</p>
    <figure class="wide">
      <LoopVideo src={img("timelapse")} poster={img("timelapse-poster.webp")} width={1920} height={1200} label="Timelapse of the agent reviewing the elevator: the chat on the left, the agent's current view on the right." />
    </figure>

    <h2 id="trust" data-toc-label="Does the model matter?">How much does the model matter?</h2>
    <p>
      The test was a disc transfer with eight known problems, like a drive that never reaches the rollers and a plate modeled
      twice. I ran the same review on it three times with each of seven models.
    </p>
    <Scoreboard />
    <p>
      The models differ in what they find past the checks and how they explain it. Opus 5.5 wrote the clearest
      comments and found about four more real problems per run, like roller spacers pushing into their bearings. Luna and Astra
      found nothing beyond the eight obvious ones.
    </p>

    <h2 id="next" data-toc-label="Writing CAD">Beyond review: writing CAD</h2>
    <p>Review reads CAD. Cadsense also writes it.</p>

    <h3>FeatureScript the agent can run</h3>
    <p>
      An AI-written FeatureScript normally goes through a slow loop. You paste it into Onshape, wait for it to fail,
      and copy the error back. Cadsense runs FeatureScript locally on Onshape's own standard library, so the agent can
      test what it writes and show you the result before anything touches Onshape.
    </p>
    <p>
      As a test, I had Claude merge two scripts which were being stitched together to model wire runs into one
      feature, then ran it on a test plate with a motor, a Spark MAX, a PDH, and a breaker.
    </p>
    <figure class="wide-sm">
      <LoopVideo src={img("harness")} poster={img("harness-poster.webp")} width={1920} height={1200} label="Three wire runs on a test plate: motor to Spark MAX, Spark MAX to PDH, and breaker to PDH." />
      <figcaption>Three runs from one feature: motor to Spark MAX, Spark MAX to PDH, and breaker to PDH.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      Any <code>.fs</code> file in a project gets a live preview with Onshape-style inputs, and it updates right away. You can build a wire by clicking points on the model.
    </p>
    <figure class="wide">
      <LoopVideo src={img("fspanel")} poster={img("fspanel-poster.webp")} width={1920} height={1200} label="Building a wire in the FeatureScript preview by clicking two waypoints on the plate, then changing the wire count and gauge." />
    </figure>

    <h3>Manufacturing part drawings</h3>
    <p>
      Pick any part in a synced assembly and Cadsense lays out a drawing sheet with views, dimensions, and hole
      callouts.
    </p>
    <figure class="wide">
      <LoopVideo src={img("drawings")} poster={img("drawings-poster.webp")} width={1920} height={1200} label="Part drawings: a shaft as a shop sheet, a formal drawing, and a traveler, then a tube and a plate, then switching to millimeters." />
    </figure>

    <h3>CAM for plates</h3>
    <p>
      Right-click a plate in the parts list and Cadsense plans its router cuts. The cuts show up as an operations table
      and a timeline you can play, so you can see the order before anything gets cut. Then export the plate as a DXF.
    </p>
    <figure class="wide">
      <LoopVideo src={img("cam")} poster={img("cam-poster.webp")} width={1920} height={1200} label="Right-clicking a plate in the parts list, opening it in CAM, and playing the router cuts: bores first, then the profile with tabs." />
    </figure>

    <h2 id="ideas">What might come next</h2>
    <p>None of these are built yet. They're ideas I'd like to try, and I want to hear which ones you'd use.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <b>Gear and belt math with ReCalc.</b> The reviewer could check gear ratios, center distances, and belt
        lengths against ReCalc instead of working them out itself.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Folder cleanup.</b> The agent could sort a messy document into folders by how each part gets made, like
        router plates, 3D prints, and COTS hardware, so parts are easy to find.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Crayola CAD.</b> The agent could sketch a first rough layout of a mechanism from a description, with
        blocks for the motors, shafts, and game piece, so you can check packaging before you CAD the real thing.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="try">Try it</h2>
    <p>
      Message me and I'll help you get set up when I release the initial beta version in the coming weeks. Before you
      do, have these ready:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>The Claude Code CLI or the Codex CLI, signed in on any plan.</li>
      <li>For Onshape, an API key that can read documents.</li>
      <li>For SolidWorks, SolidWorks installed on the same computer.</li>
    </ul>
    <figure class="wide">
      <LoopVideo src={img("link")} poster={img("link-poster.webp")} width={1920} height={1200} label="Adding an Onshape project from a link, then typing a review request in the chat and opening the model picker." />
    </figure>

  </article>
</PostLayout>

<style>
  .post {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 24px 20px 0;
  }

  .post-head {
    margin: 32px 0 28px;
    padding-bottom: 24px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .kicker {
    margin: 0 0 14px;
    font: 12px var(--font-mono);
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  h1 {
    margin: 0 0 16px;
    font-size: clamp(30px, 5vw, 42px);
    line-height: 1.12;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .dek {
    margin: 0 0 18px;
    font-size: 19px;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: #c9c9c9;
  }

  .byline {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 14px;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .post > p,
  .post > ul,
  .post > ol,
  .soon-section > p {
    margin: 0 0 18px;
    font-size: 17px;
    line-height: 1.72;
  }

  .post li {
    margin-bottom: 6px;
  }

  .post b {
    color: var(--text);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  h2 {
    margin: 64px 0 16px;
    font-size: 26px;
    line-height: 1.25;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--text);
    scroll-margin-top: 72px;
  }


  h3 {
    margin: 36px 0 8px;
    font-size: 20px;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--text);
  }

  blockquote {
    margin: 0 0 18px;
    padding: 2px 0 2px 16px;
    border-left: 2px solid #4a4a4a;
    font-size: 17px;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: #d6d6d6;
  }

  blockquote p {
    margin: 0;
  }

  code {
    font: 15px var(--font-mono);
    background: #141414;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 4px;
    padding: 1px 5px;
  }

  figure {
    margin: 32px 0;
  }

  figure img {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border-radius: 6px;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    background: #262626;
  }

  figcaption {
    margin-top: 10px;
    font: 13px/1.55 var(--font-mono);
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .wide,
  .wide-sm {
    width: min(1080px, calc(100vw - 32px));
    margin-left: 50%;
    transform: translateX(-50%);
  }

  .wide-sm {
    width: min(860px, calc(100vw - 32px));
  }


  /* The box reaches past the column so its text lines up with the body text around it. */
  .soon-section {
    position: relative;
    margin: 56px -25px 24px;
    padding: 28px 24px 8px;
    border: 1px dashed rgba(253, 230, 138, 0.55);
    border-radius: 8px;
  }

  .soon-section > h2 {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .soon-tag {
    position: absolute;
    top: -11px;
    left: 18px;
    padding: 2px 10px;
    font: 600 11.5px var(--font-mono);
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--marker);
    background: var(--canvas);
  }

  .narrow {
    max-width: 480px;
    margin-inline: auto;
  }

  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .soon-section {
      margin-inline: -12px;
      padding-inline: 11px;
    }
  }

  /* Screenshots of GitHub review comments, stacked in one card with GitHub's dark background. */
  .reviews {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1px;
    overflow: hidden;
    background: var(--border);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 8px;
  }

  .reviews img {
    display: block;
    border: 0;
    border-radius: 0;
    background: #0d1117;
  }

  .gap {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 12px;
    margin: 0 0 18px;
  }

  .gap > div {
    padding: 14px 16px;
    background: var(--chrome);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 8px;
  }

  .gap h3 {
    margin: 0 0 8px;
    font-size: 15px;
  }

  .gap ul {
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: 18px;
    font-size: 15.5px;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .gap > div:last-child {
    border-color: rgba(253, 230, 138, 0.45);
  }

  .pair {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 10px;
  }

  .post > .status {
    margin: -2px 0 12px;
    font: 12.5px var(--font-mono);
    color: var(--text-dim);
  }

  .status a {
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }


  @media (max-width: 760px) {



    .pair,
    .gap {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
